<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I Need Five Yucas. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> I Need Five Yucas.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Participate in role-plays based on classroom scenarios.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Speaking: Can describe the quantity of items needed using numbers (e.g., 'I need five...').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can participate in role-plays by responding to questions about items and prices in a market setting (e.g., 'How many apples do you need?' 'Three apples, please.').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can ask for prices using correct structures, for example, using simple WH-questions and phrases in a role-play (e.g., 'How much is the pineapple?' 'It is one dollar.').</li>
                </p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0e6ea; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task<br/>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and displays a "market stand" (could be a table with picture cards or realia like fruits/vegetables: pineapple, cassava, potatoes). The teacher points to items and asks, "What is this?" and "Do you like [pineapple]?" to activate prior knowledge. Students respond in chorus or individually with single words. The teacher then holds up a few items, e.g., three potatoes, and says, "Three potatoes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a pineapple and pronounces /p/ in 'pineapple' clearly, then holds up play money and pronounces /m/ in 'money'. The teacher models a simple dialogue: "Hello! I need five yucas." (holding up five fingers). "How much is it?" "It is two dollars." The teacher uses gestures for 'need', 'how much', and counting. The teacher repeats the dialogue slowly, emphasizing intonation for questions and quantities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I buying or selling?" (Expected: Buying)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I ask 'how much' for fruit or for price?" (Expected: For price)</li>
                </ul>
                The teacher shows examples of "shopping list" on the board with simple drawings for reference.
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated dialogue between a customer and a vendor at a market. The dialogue will feature the target vocabulary (market, price, item, pineapple, cassava, potatoes, money, buy, need, want, how much, how many, numbers 1-100) and grammar (WH-Questions, Quantities, Present Simple).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Illustrated dialogue on a large flashcard or projected on the board.
                <br><b>Dialogue example:</b>
                <br><b>Customer:</b> "Excuse me, I need three pineapples. How much are they?"
                <br><b>Vendor:</b> "Three pineapples are four dollars. They are fresh!"
                <br><b>Customer:</b> "Okay, I want three pineapples and two yucas."
                <br><b>Vendor:</b> "How many yucas do you need?"
                <br><b>Customer:</b> "Two yucas, please. How much is the total?"
                <br><b>Vendor:</b> "That is six dollars. Thank you!"
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will be able to engage in simple, direct exchanges about personal needs and immediate topics related to shopping, specifically asking for quantities and prices.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "Where are they?" (Expected: At the market). "What are they buying?" (Expected: Pineapple, yucas).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> "The customer needs five pineapples." (False). "The yucas are delicious." (False, the dialogue says pineapples are fresh).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match the item with its price, e.g., 'three pineapples' with 'four dollars'.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Speaking.</b> Students will practice the target structures with increasing independence to ensure correct usage and pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Asking about prices ('How much is the...?'), describing quantities ('I need [number] [item].'), using polite expressions ('Excuse me, please.'). Grammar: WH-Questions, Quantities, Present Simple. Vocabulary: market, price, pineapple, cassava, potatoes, money, buy, need, want, numbers 1-100.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice/Choral Repetition:</b> The teacher writes key phrases on the board: "I need [number] [item]." "How much is the [item]?" "It is [number] dollars." Students repeat after the teacher, focusing on the /p/ and /m/ sounds and the blending of number words (e.g., 'one' /w/ /ʌn/). The teacher uses flashcards to prompt quantity and item changes, e.g., "I need *two* *potatoes*."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled substitution drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of picture cards for items (pineapple, cassava, potatoes) and number cards (1-10). Student A picks an item and a number card. Student A says, "I want [number] [item]." Student B responds, "How much is [number] [item]?" Student A (or prompted by teacher with a price) says, "It is [number] dollars." They swap roles. The teacher circulates, correcting pronunciation and grammar.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Information Gap (Pair Work):</b> Students are given "Shopping List A" and "Shopping List B." Each list has different items with quantities or prices missing. For example, List A might have "I need 3 yucas. Price: ?" and List B has "I need ? pineapples. Price: $5." Students ask each other questions to complete their lists: "How many pineapples do you need?" "I need four pineapples." "How much are the yucas?" They use polite expressions like "Excuse me..." and "Please..." The focus is on using target questions and quantity responses.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-17 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</b> This stage focuses on fluency and authentic communication, allowing students to apply all learned structures and vocabulary in a natural way.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Role-play. Students will work in pairs or small groups (customer, vendor, cashier) to act out a shopping scenario at a mini "market."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Setting up the Market:</b> The classroom is transformed into a "market." The teacher sets up different "stalls" (tables with item cards/realia: yucas, pineapples, potatoes, other fruits/vegetables introduced in previous lessons). Each stall has a simple price list.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Role Assignment:</b> Students are assigned roles: "Customer," "Vendor," or "Cashier." Customers receive a simple "shopping list" (e.g., "I need 5 yucas. I want 2 pineapples."). Vendors stand at their stalls. Cashiers have play money.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Role-Play:</b> Customers move to different stalls. They must initiate the interaction using polite expressions, state what they need (e.g., "Excuse me, I need five yucas."), ask for prices ("How much are the yucas?"), and respond to vendor questions ("How many yucas do you need?"). Vendors describe items (e.g., "They are fresh!" "Delicious!") and state prices. Cashiers receive money and confirm the total. The students focus on expressing needs and asking/giving prices, using numbers 1-100, and the discourse marker "and" to combine needs (e.g., "I need two apples and four bananas").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>4. Rotation:</b> After a few minutes, students rotate roles so everyone gets a chance to be a customer, vendor, and cashier. The teacher observes, providing minimal error correction, focusing on fluency and communication.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play. The teacher uses a simple rubric to check for the use of target language by selected students or during a final mini-role-play for all students.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their role-play interactions for a brief moment, focusing on a clear request and a price query/response. For example, one pair can quickly demonstrate their interaction to the class.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially /p/, /m/, and numbers).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., market, pineapple, cassava, money, buy, need, want, how much, how many).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of WH-questions for prices and quantities.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to describe quantities using numbers (e.g., 'I need five yucas').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of polite expressions (e.g., 'Excuse me,' 'please').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and engagement in the role-play.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket before leaving class.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned to say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you ask for the price of a pineapple?" (Yes/No with a sentence example).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of shopping at the market was easy to talk about?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was difficult, and what can you practice more?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The role-play activity worked well as students were engaged and used the target language effectively. The information gap activity was particularly useful for prompting natural question-and-answer practice. Some students still struggled with the correct intonation for 'How much is it?' and remembering to use 's' for plural items when asking about quantities (e.g., 'three pineapples'). I need to continue reinforcing the plural 's' and question intonation in the next lessons. I might need to provide more visual aids for numbers 1-100 or a simple calculator prop for cashiers to aid in price calculations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> For Lesson 4 (Writing Composition), students will use the vocabulary and grammatical structures from today's speaking lesson to write their own shopping lists and short dialogues about buying items at the market. They will practice writing quantities and prices clearly, preparing them to compose simple sentences and paragraphs based on the "Shopping at the Market" scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>